artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a relativistic heavy-ion collision modeled by relativistic hydrodynamics, in which the evolution of the strongly interacting fluid is governed by local conservation of energy–momentum and (where appropriate) conserved charges, together with an equation of state. To compare with experiment, one must convert the hydrodynamic solution into a final spectrum of free-streaming hadrons.

In the standard Cooper–Frye prescription one fixes a sharp three-dimensional freeze-out hypersurface Σ (typically a surface of constant temperature T_f) and writes the invariant momentum distribution of species i as
  E dN_i/d^3p = (g_i/(2π)^3) ∫_Σ p^μ dΣ_μ f_i(p·u(x), T(x), μ_i(x)),
where u^μ is the fluid four-velocity on Σ and f_i is the local equilibrium (Jüttner) distribution. This sudden-freeze-out picture is known to suffer from: (i) violation of exact energy–momentum and charge conservation across Σ when Σ has space-like portions; (ii) "negative-flux" contributions from regions of Σ where p^μ dΣ_μ < 0, representing particles flowing back into the fluid; and (iii) artificial entropy production at the discontinuous transition from interacting fluid to free streaming.

In a continuous-emission scenario, particles instead escape gradually throughout the expansion with a momentum- and position-dependent escape probability, so that emission occurs over a broad range of temperatures rather than a single T_f. Both extremes—the sharp Cooper–Frye surface and the continuous emission with a nearly flat emission-temperature distribution—are observed to reproduce measured single-particle spectra and anisotropic flow coefficients essentially equally well.

Problem. Formulate a particle-production prescription that converts a given relativistic hydrodynamic solution into final hadron spectra such that:
1. exact local conservation of energy, momentum and conserved charges between fluid and emitted particles is respected;
2. no negative-flux or unphysical entropy-production artifacts arise;
3. the prescription interpolates consistently between (or replaces) the sudden Cooper–Frye and continuous-emission limits;
4. it produces observables (single-particle spectra, anisotropic flow harmonics, HBT radii) that match experimental data and uniquely discriminate between freeze-out scenarios that currently fit the data equally well.
